---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { absoluteUrl, seo } from '../../data/seo';

const meta = seo.aiReliability;
const siteUrl = Astro.site?.toString() || 'https://tensor-standards-consortium.org/';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: meta.title,
  description: meta.description,
  url: absoluteUrl(siteUrl, meta.canonicalPath),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={meta.title}
  description={meta.description}
  canonicalPath={meta.canonicalPath}
  ogType={meta.ogType}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="container prose-card">
    <h2>Why the Same Prompt Produces Different Answers</h2>
    <p>
      Large language models sample tokens from probability distributions. Even with identical prompts,
      early token variation can shift later reasoning paths.
    </p>

    <div class="signal-grid">
      <article class="signal-card">
        <h3>Stochastic Decoding</h3>
        <p>Sampling methods improve diversity, but they introduce run-to-run variance.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="signal-card">
        <h3>Path Dependence</h3>
        <p>Small early differences compound over long responses.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="signal-card">
        <h3>Prompt Ambiguity</h3>
        <p>Broad instructions allow multiple valid continuations with different operational outcomes.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h2>Research Baseline</h2>
    <ol class="citation-list">
      <li>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          Brown et al. (2020), Language Models are Few-Shot Learners
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.09751" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          Holtzman et al. (2019), The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.11171" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          Wang et al. (2022), Self-Consistency Improves Chain of Thought Reasoning
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.07958" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          Lin et al. (2021), TruthfulQA
        </a>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why TENSOR Helps</h2>
    <p>
      TENSOR does not remove model stochasticity. It constrains control flow with deterministic graph semantics,
      so decisions are explicit and auditable.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Stable question IDs:</strong> model outputs map to fixed investigation nodes.</li>
      <li><strong>Explicit branch outcomes:</strong> transitions resolve to <code>yes</code>, <code>no</code>, or <code>unknown</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Replayable traversal:</strong> incident review can reconstruct path decisions.</li>
      <li><strong>Adjudication boundary:</strong> model reasoning supports decisions, but does not define branch policy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Integration Pattern for Operators</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Run multiple model passes for high-impact investigative nodes.</li>
      <li>Adjudicate with explicit rules or self-consistency before branch transition.</li>
      <li>Persist candidate outputs in <code>extensions</code> for post-incident review.</li>
      <li>Escalate unresolved disagreement as <code>unknown</code> for analyst review.</li>
    </ol>

    <blockquote>
      AI can accelerate analysis. The control plane must remain deterministic.
      TENSOR keeps that boundary explicit.
    </blockquote>

    <p class="hub-links">
      Implement next:
      <a href="/implement/">Implementation guide</a>
      ·
      <a href="/standards/">Standards policy</a>
      ·
      <a href="/conformance/">Conformance suite</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
